+### `on_native_session_event`
+
+Observe canonical native-session outcomes and real pending prompts independently
+of attached viewers. A concrete consumer is an optional notification plugin:
+it can persist encrypted delivery work without wrapping gateway internals.
+Register with `ctx.register_hook("on_native_session_event", callback)` and probe
+`getattr(ctx, "native_session_events_version", 0)` before enabling the feature.
+The capability is also available in the isolated plugin host. The documented
+`hermes_cli.plugins.NATIVE_SESSION_EVENTS_VERSION` constant exposes the same
+local payload version to a separately loaded dashboard backend.
+
+The callback receives `event: dict` (and additive telemetry keyword fields).
+Version 1 contains:
+
+| Field | Meaning |
+| --- | --- |
+| `v` | `1`, the local event contract version |
+| `type` | `turn.completed`, `turn.failed`, `turn.interrupted`, `turn.unknown`, `request.waiting`, or `request.resolved` |
+| `event_id` | Stable SHA-256 identity over the canonical coordinates, outcome type and optional prompt; replay-safe, not a delivery guarantee |
+| `profile_id` | Served profile label (`default` for the root), never its filesystem home |
+| `root_id`, `stored_id` | Canonical authority root and current physical compression tip |
+| `turn_id`, `execution_generation` | Committed admission identity and its exact execution generation |
+| `request_id` | Actual native prompt ID for waiting/resolved events, otherwise null |
+| `kind` | `reply`, `failure`, `approval`, or `question`; unknown execution is failure-category attention, not a fabricated failed outcome |
+| `meaningful` | Whether this fact represents visible attention; interrupted and successful quiet turns are false; resolved requests cancel earlier waiting work |
+| `is_subagent` | Whether the stored session is explicitly classified as a subagent |
+| `title`, `preview` | Profile label and safe preview, redacted in the owning profile and bounded to 160/1,024 UTF-8 bytes; approval commands, responses and raw errors are excluded |
+| `created_at` | Unix seconds when this observation was captured |
+| `expires_at` | Waiting/resolved request expiry (same value for the pair), bounded by its timeout or five minutes |
+
+Terminal observations follow committed authority settlement, including failures
+before an agent runs. A lost managed worker emits `turn.unknown` only after its
+unknown state is committed. Actual native pending controls produce request
+observations; smart approvals and mere tool-call attempts do not. A request
+may resolve immediately, so consumers must cancel queued waiting alerts.
+Unstarted queued cancellations are not completed execution events.
+
+Callbacks use the existing bounded, per-consumer observer dispatcher off the
+authority path. Each consumer receives its own payload copy and owning-profile
+context. Hook return values never alter execution and never mark a session as
+viewed or delivered. A slow consumer can exhaust its queue, and observers are
+not a durable journal: persist accepted work promptly and reconcile canonical
+state on reconnect. An observer cannot promise exactly-once or always-visible
+delivery, and notification infrastructure must remain opt-in. Local sessions
+whose frozen policy bypasses user configuration (including safe mode) emit no
+profile-plugin observations.
+
 ### `on_session_end`
 
 Fires at the **very end** of every `run_conversation()` call, regardless of outcome. Also fires from the CLI's exit handler if the agent was mid-turn when the user quit.
